<Base title="Research">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="label">Research statement</p>
    <h1>Technology in the places it was not designed for</h1>
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Technology is designed in large, stable rooms and used in small, moving, improvised ones. My research follows it there. I ask what the design assumed, what the setting does to that assumption, and what a system built for the setting would look like. The answer is usually an artefact, so I build it, put it back in the field, and study it again. Twice that loop has produced a company, which I treat as the most durable kind of field site: a place where the questions come from teachers, parents, and players rather than from the literature.</p>
      <p>Methodologically I move between qualitative field work, phenomenological analysis, design studies, and quantitative evaluation at scale, the last of which I learned in industry and now teach. Three threads run through the work.</p>
    </div></div>
  </header>

  <Section label="Threads" title="Research program">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <div class="thread">
        <p class="label">01 · Constrained and improvised spaces</p>
        <h3>What small, moving places do to design assumptions</h3>
        <p>The doctoral work. A field study of the #vanlife community, who live and work in vehicles with technology built for houses (CHI 2021); a phenomenological account of how people make places in small spaces and what technology does to that (Graphics Interface 2024); and a thesis that draws the design implications together. Next: cockpits and other instrumented small spaces, where I hold the domain knowledge as a licensed commercial pilot.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="thread">
        <p class="label">02 · Programmable physical objects for play</p>
        <h3>How much can you change a familiar object before it stops being familiar</h3>
        <p>Proteus, a deck of cards whose faces are e-ink displays, built to study familiarity as a design resource (CHI PLAY 2024, CHI PLAY 2026). Three provisional patents on what the deck can do that a printed one cannot. Now Scramble Deck, a company, which means the next studies can run with hundreds of decks rather than five. See the <a href="/work/scramble/">path</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="thread">
        <p class="label">03 · Wellbeing communication in schools</p>
        <h3>Telling a parent something hard without the report becoming a label</h3>
        <p>A design study of parent-facing dashboards in Indian schools, where wellbeing screening carries stigma (ACM Interactive Health 2026). Now Bodhika Labs, an assessment company with a partner school in Hyderabad, and with it a methods paper on how access to field sites compounds across studies (Graphics Interface 2026). Next: teacher decision-making from the same dashboards. See the <a href="/work/bodhika/">path</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Companies and research" title="How the two relate">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Scramble Deck and Bodhika Labs came out of the research and I hold founder positions in both. I keep them in the open: studies involving people run under research ethics review, the companies are disclosed in the papers, and the research questions are the ones I'd pursue whether or not the companies existed. What the companies add is access, scale, and a reason for the artefact to keep existing after the paper is published.</p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

